@.str.35 = private unnamed_addr constant [1748 x i8] c"The Cyclictest Benchmark Utility\0AUsage:\0A  -c --clock [CLOCK]: selects the clock: 0 selects CLOCK_REALTIME, 1 selects CLOCK_MONOTONIC (default).\0A  -d --distance [US]: The distance of thread intervals. Default is 500us.\0A  -D --duration [TIME]: Test duration length in seconds. Default is 0 (endless).\0A  -e --help: Display this help and quit.\0A  -h --histogram [US]: Output the histogram data to stdout. US is the maximum value to be printed.\0A  -H --histofall: Same as -h except that an additional histogram column\0A     is displayed at the right that contains summary data of all thread histograms.\0A     If cyclictest runs a single thread only, the -H option is equivalent to -h.\0A  -i --interval [US]: The thread interval. Default is 1000us.\0A  -l --loops [N]: The number of measurement loops. Default is 0 (endless).\0A  -m --measurement [METHOD]: Set the time measurement method:\0A     0 selects clock_gettime, 1 uses the NuttX timer API.\0A     WARNING:\0A       If METHOD 1 is selected, you need to specify a timer device (e.g. /dev/timer0) in -T.\0A  -n --nanosleep [METHOD]: Set the waiting method: 0 selects clock_nanosleep,\0A     1 waits for the POLLIN flag on a timer device. Default is 0.\0A     WARNING:\0A       Choosing 1 works only with one thread, the -t value is therefore set to 1.\0A       If METHOD 1 is selected, you need to specify a timer device (e.g. /dev/timer0) in -T.\0A  -p --prio: Set the priority of the first thread.\0A  -q --quiet: Print a summary only on exit.\0A  -t --threads [N]: The number of test threads to be created. Default is 1.\0A  -T --timer-device [DEV]: The measuring timer device.\0A     Must be specified when -m=1 or -n=1.\0A  -y --policy [NAME]: Set the scheduler policy, where NAME is \0A     fifo, rr, batch, idle, normal, other.\0A\00", align 1
@.str.36 = private unnamed_addr constant [23 x i8] c"Specify timer device!\0A\00", align 1
@.str.37 = private unnamed_addr constant [15 x i8] c"TCIOC_GETSTAUS\00", align 1
